+1.3 Use a normalized tubular chart $\Phi$ of $K$ as in [F4] and [F9], and write $\Phi^{-1}(c(p,t))=(b_t(p),v_t(p))$, with $v_t(p)$ in the normal fibre over $b_t(p)$. Compactness permits one small interval of $t$ on which these expressions are defined for every $p$. At $t=0$ they are $(p,0)$, and normalization identifies the vertical derivative with $a(p)s(p)$. The maps $b_t$ are $C^1$-close to the identity. In a smooth oriented circle coordinate their lifts have the form $u+r_t(u)$ with $r_t$ periodic and $\|r_t'\|_\infty<1/2$ for small $t$; thus the lifts are increasing degree-one diffeomorphisms. Hence each $b_t$ is an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism. Reparametrize the level over $x=b_t(p)$, obtaining a smooth normal section $w_t(x)=v_t(b_t^{-1}(x))$. Taylor expansion in finitely many bundle charts gives, uniformly on $K$, $w_t(x)=t a(x)s(x)+O(t^2)$. Because $a$ has positive minimum and $s$ is unit, after decreasing $\delta$ this implies $\langle w_\delta(x),s(x)\rangle>0$ for every $x$. [given, F4, F9, step 1.2, construct, calculus]
